 ### [Camera Preconditioning](12545-camera-preconditioning.md)
-Camera Preconditioning (CamP) is a technique that normalizes and decorrelates camera parameters during Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) optimization by transforming the optimization problem from direct camera parameters to latent preconditioned parameters. It addresses the ill-conditioning caused by mixed units (e.g., translation in world units vs. focal length in pixels) and spurious correlations between parameters, which cause naive optimization to converge slowly or inaccurately. Unlike per-parameter scaling or adaptive optimizers like Adam, which may ignore sensitivity differences or fail to decouple correlated coefficients, CamP uses a preconditioner derived from the covariance of projection derivatives to ensure uniform sensitivity across all parameter dimensions.
+Camera Preconditioning (CamP) is a technique to normalize and decorrelate camera parameters during Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) optimization by transforming the optimization problem from direct camera parameters $\boldsymbol{\phi}_i$ to latent preconditioned parameters $\tilde{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_i$, using a preconditioner matrix $P_i^{-1}$ derived from the covariance of projection derivatives. It addresses issues where parameters are correlated or have different units (e.g., translation in world units vs. focal length in pixels), which causes ill-conditioning in naive optimization. Unlike per-parameter scaling or adaptive optimizers like Adam, CamP decouples highly correlated coefficients by using the full covariance matrix, ensuring that parameter updates are scaled according to their sensitivity to the rendered image.
 
 ### [VMRF (View Matching Neural Radiance Fields)](12630-vmrf-view-matching-neural-radiance-fields.md)
 VMRF is a method for novel view synthesis that learns Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) representations without prior knowledge of camera poses or hand-crafted camera pose distributions. It addresses the failure of classical Structure-from-Motion (SfM) in low-texture scenes and the instability of absolute pose estimation methods (like GNeRF) when out-of-dataset views are present. Unlike GNeRF, which estimates absolute poses directly from images and requires accurate pose distributions for training its inversion network, VMRF estimates **relative pose transformations** between a rendered image (with a random pose) and a real image using a view matching scheme based on unbalanced optimal transport (UOT). This approach makes VMRF robust to out-of-dataset views and removes the dependency on prior pose distribution knowledge.
 
+### [SPARF](12705-sparf.md)
+SPARF is a joint pose-NeRF training strategy designed for novel view synthesis from sparse input views (as few as 3) with noisy camera pose estimates. It solves the problems of overfitting and geometric inconsistency inherent in sparse-view regimes by enforcing multi-view geometry constraints, specifically multi-view correspondence and depth consistency losses, rather than relying solely on photometric loss. Unlike sibling methods that optimize poses and NeRF weights using independent per-image photometric supervision, SPARF explicitly connects training views through pixel correspondences to drive convergence to a globally accurate pose and scene solution.
+
+### [NeRF Pose and Appearance Optimization](13113-nerf-pose-and-appearance-optimization.md)
+This method jointly optimizes camera poses and neural scene representations for unconstrained image collections containing varying illumination and transient occluders, without requiring camera pose priors. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by introducing a "candidate head" with image-dependent embeddings to handle "hard-pose" images during initial training, combined with a loss scheduling mechanism that gradually transitions from candidate-specific to shared scene representations.
+
+### [Test-Time Two-Stage Optimization](13119-test-time-two-stage-optimization.md)
+Test-Time Two-Stage Optimization is a post-training evaluation procedure designed to refine camera poses and appearance embeddings for unconstrained image collections with varying lighting and transient objects. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by decoupling pose refinement and appearance adaptation into two sequential stages, addressing the limitations of methods that optimize only poses (e.g., BARF) or only appearance (e.g., NeRF-W).
+
