 ## Background
-The problem of pose estimation in the context of Neural Radiance Fields originated with the need to remove the dependency on external Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines like COLMAP, which can fail on textureless or repetitive scenes. Early work, **NeRF–** (February 2021) [S1], jointly estimated NeRF model parameters and camera parameters, allowing for end-to-end radiance field construction. However, it was limited to front-facing scenes and struggled with rotational motion. Concurrently, **BARF** (Bundle-Adjusted Neural Radiance Field, April 2021) [S1] introduced coarse-to-fine registration by adaptively masking positional encoding, outperforming NeRF– on LLFF forward-facing scenes. **iNeRF** (December 2020) [S1] formulated pose reconstruction as an inverse problem, optimizing the pose of a pre-trained NeRF using photometric loss and interest-point sampling. **SCNeRF** (Self-Calibrating NeRF, August 2021) [S1] extended this to optimize intrinsic parameters for non-linear models like fisheye lenses using curriculum learning. More recent methods like **MELON** [S1], **VMRF** [S1], and **RoDynRF** [S1] address specific challenges such as symmetry, unbalanced transport for occlusions, and dynamic scenes, respectively.
+The problem of joint pose and NeRF optimization emerged as a solution to the dependency on accurate Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines, which can fail on textureless or repetitive surfaces. Early work, **NeRF–** (February 2021), jointly estimated NeRF model parameters and camera parameters, achieving comparable results to COLMAP-based baselines but struggling with rotational motion [S1]. Concurrently, **BARF** (April 2021) introduced coarse-to-fine registration by adaptively masking positional encoding, outperforming NeRF– on LLFF forward-facing scenes [S1]. **SCNeRF** (August 2021) extended this to self-calibration, optimizing intrinsic parameters for non-linear models like fisheye lenses using curriculum learning [S1]. **iNeRF** (December 2020) formulated pose reconstruction as an inverse problem given a pre-trained NeRF, using photo-metric loss and interest-point sampling [S1]. More recent methods like **MELON** address symmetry-induced local minima in $SO(3)$, while **VMRF** uses unbalanced optimal transport for robust relative pose estimation without prior pose distributions [S1].
 
